
Note :- 
Important file checking operators in bash script if writing ?

    Operator	Meaning
        -f	Checks regular file exists
        -d	Checks directory exists
        -e	Checks whether file or directory exists
        -r	Checks read permission
        -w	Checks write permission
        -x	Checks execute permission
        -s	Checks file exists and is not empty

ii)
Left side of assignment → No $.

eg :- for (( $num1=0; $num1<11; $num1++ ))  //wrong
      for (( num1=0; num1<11; num1++ ))   //right 

iii) 
result=$(date)
echo "$result"

date  //will print date but above "$( ) is command substitution. It captures command output"

Remember: $ is optional when reading variables inside (( )), but don't use it on the left side of an assignment.

[[ ]] — Advanced conditions in Bash
eg :- if [[ "$name" == P* ]]; then

iv) 
Quick revision table
Syntax	Purpose	                       Example
( )	Subshell	              (cd /tmp)
$( )	Capture command output	      x=$(date)
(( ))	Arithmetic	              (( a > b ))  ((a++))
[ ]	Basic conditions	      [ -f file.txt ]
[[ ]]	Advanced Bash conditions      [[ $name == P* ]]
{ }	Group commands	             { echo hi; echo hello; }

v) quick revision of wc :
 
wc 'filename'     --> will give output in [lines] [words] [bytes/chars] filename

wc -l 'filename'  --> to fetch only lines

wc -w 'filename'  --> to fetch only words

wc -m 'filename'  --> to fetch only chars/bytes

vi)  if conditions 
Syntax	Purpose
if command; then	        Checks command exit status
if [ condition ]; then	        Basic condition checking
if [[ condition ]]; then	Advanced Bash condition
if (( condition )); then	Arithmetic condition


